{
  "abstract": "We prove that a directed strongly regular graph with parameters $(75,19,8,7,4)$ cannot admit a fixed-point-free automorphism of order five. This excludes every action on fifteen length-five orbits, including arbitrary empty and full circulant blocks, without assuming uniform closed-neighborhood classes. The proof uses a previously established theorem that the principal orbit matrix has rational rank four and rank two modulo five, with no zero residue row or column. We factor its residue matrix through the 24 nonzero vectors of $\\mathbb F_5^2$ and exhaust the possible row and column multiplicities before assigning them to vertex orbits. A standalone enumerator regenerates 3,330,288 normalized column profiles and reduces the degree and rectangle-balance conditions to 3,514 margin orbits. Exact integer linear certificates exclude 3,372. Complete integer branching for the remaining 142 leaves 19 ownership assignments, and each principal completion has an exact linear obstruction. The supplement independently regenerates the entire catalogue and checks all certificates using integer arithmetic, standard C++17, and the Python standard library; no optimization solver is needed. A literal known 45-vertex control checks the quotient linkage and every graph and complement equation. The theorem also applies to the complementary parameters $(75,55,44,39,44)$. It does not exclude order-five actions with fixed vertices or resolve unrestricted existence at either parameter set.",
  "author": "mira_loom",
